# AWS-Project
Installing and Launching an Apache Webserver from an Amazon EC2 Instance

Scenario:
Your company wants to start shifting from using on premises servers to using servers in the cloud. Rather than purchasing all the infrastructure in a data center they ask you to create an EC2 instance in AWS to host their new website.

An Amazon EC2 instance is a virtual server in Amazon’s Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) for running applications on the Amazon Web Services (AWS) infrastructure. Many businesses are migrating to the use of these virtual machines due to the many benefits they provide, such as reliability, security, flexibility, and cost saving.

Prerequisites:
A free AWS account set up with your email address
An IAM user account with administrative permissions and access keys
Objectives:
Launch an EC2 Amazon Linux t2.micro (free tier) in a public subnet of your Default VPC.
Create a security group that allows inbound traffic on HTTP for 0.0.0.0/0 and allows inbound traffic on SSH from your IP address.
SSH into your EC2 instance and install Apache with a custom webpage using a BASH script.

Step 1: Open the Amazon EC2 Console and choose a Region
To begin, search EC2 in the search bar and click EC2 to access the Amazon EC2 console.
In the navigation bar at the top of the screen, select a Region to launch the instance. We will be using the US East (N. Virginia) us-east-1 Region. Be mindful when choosing your Region because not every Amazon EC2 resource can be shared between Regions.

Step 2: Configure the EC2 instance
Under the Launch an Instance page, scroll down to Name and tag. Give your instance a name that correlates to what you are using it for. This will help you identify your instance later.
Under Application and OS Images (Amazon Machine Image), choose Amazon Linux and click the drop down to select Amazon Linux 2 AMI (HVM)- Kernel 5.10, SSD Volume Type. This will ensure you are using the free tier eligible product.
Under Instance type, choose t2.micro which also falls under the free tier.

A key pair is essential to be able to connect to your instance securely, so be sure to complete this next step. Scroll down to key pair (login), and either choose an existing key pair or create a new one. We will be creating a new one, so click on Create new key pair.

Give your key pair a name. Leave the Key pair type as RSA and the Private key file format as .pem. Then click Create key pair. The private key will automatically download to your Downloads folder on your computer, but you can relocate it to another folder for easier access if you like.

Under Network settings, leave the Network and Subnet as it is. This allows you to choose the default VPC and allows instances in a default subnet to receive a public IPv4 address.

Under Firewall (security groups), click the box Allow SSH traffic from and use the dropdown to select My IP. This allows inbound traffic on SSH from your IP address.

Next, click the box Allow HTTP traffic from the internet to allow inbound traffic on HTTP for 0.0.0.0/0.

You do not need to change anything under Configure storage, Advanced details, or Summary. Go ahead and click Launch instance.

You will see a message to show that your launch was successful.
To check to see if your instance has finished spinning up, click on Instances in the lefthand sidebar. It will take several moments for the status to update to running, so grab a snack while you wait.

