Step 4: Install Apache with a custom webpage using a BASH script
By default, your EC2 instance will not have any permissions to do anything, so we will have to give it the permissions of the user by using the sudo command.

We will begin by running the following command to open the text editor, Vim, and create a file for our script. We’ll call our file bashscript.

sudo vim bashscript
Once the text editor is open, type i to enter insert mode. Then, we’ll input the following script:

To write and exit the script, hit Esc and :wq.

If you try to run the script from this point, you will find that you are not able to. This is because our EC2 instance does not have permission to execute. We have to give it the permissions of the user by running the following command:

sudo chmod 755 bashscript

Now, you can run the script. This may take a few moments.

sudo ./bashscript

This screen confirms that Apache has been installed successfully. Let’s check the status of our page.

sudo systemctl status httpd

Everything looks good, but we need to verify it is running on the web browser. We’ll need our IP address to do this. We’ll return to the AWS console to copy our IP address.

Time to test the page. Paste the IP address into the URL bar.

